{"subjectId":"c","id":"math-ctype-time","title":"math.h, ctype.h & time.h","group":"Standard Library, Modern C & Interview","order":54,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Three small headers you reach for constantly: floating-point maths, character classification, and dates &amp; times.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math\"></i>math.h</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — macOS output (Linux glibc may print -nan for sqrt(-1))</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;math.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"sqrt=%.4f pow=%.0f cbrt=%.1f hypot=%.1f\\n\", sqrt(2.0), pow(2, 10), cbrt(27), hypot(3, 4));\n    printf(\"floor=%.0f ceil=%.0f round=%.0f trunc=%.0f\\n\", floor(-2.5), ceil(-2.5), round(-2.5), trunc(-2.5));\n    printf(\"fabs=%.1f fmod=%.1f\\n\", fabs(-3.5), fmod(10.5, 3));\n    printf(\"exp(1)=%.5f log=%.4f log10=%.1f log2=%.1f\\n\", exp(1), log(10), log10(1000), log2(8));\n    double pi = acos(-1.0);\n    printf(\"pi=%.6f sin(pi/6)=%.2f atan2(1,1)=%.4f\\n\", pi, sin(pi / 6), atan2(1, 1));\n    printf(\"%f %f %d %d\\n\", sqrt(-1.0), INFINITY, isnan(sqrt(-1.0)), isinf(-INFINITY));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>sqrt=1.4142 pow=1024 cbrt=3.0 hypot=5.0\nfloor=-3 ceil=-2 round=-3 trunc=-2\nfabs=3.5 fmod=1.5\nexp(1)=2.71828 log=2.3026 log10=3.0 log2=3.0\npi=3.141593 sin(pi/6)=0.50 atan2(1,1)=0.7854\nnan inf 1 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Trig functions take <strong>radians</strong>. <code>abs()</code> is for <code>int</code> — <code>abs(-2.5)</code> silently truncates; use <code>fabs</code> for doubles. On Linux, link with <code>-lm</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-abc\"></i>ctype.h</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *s = \"Hi 42_x!\";\n    for (const char *p = s; *p; p++) {\n        unsigned char c = (unsigned char)*p;        /* the cast avoids UB for negative chars */\n        printf(\"'%c' alpha=%d digit=%d space=%d punct=%d upper=%d -&gt; '%c'\\n\",\n               c, !!isalpha(c), !!isdigit(c), !!isspace(c), !!ispunct(c), !!isupper(c), toupper(c));\n    }\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>'H' alpha=1 digit=0 space=0 punct=0 upper=1 -&gt; 'H'\n'i' alpha=1 digit=0 space=0 punct=0 upper=0 -&gt; 'I'\n' ' alpha=0 digit=0 space=1 punct=0 upper=0 -&gt; ' '\n'4' alpha=0 digit=1 space=0 punct=0 upper=0 -&gt; '4'\n'2' alpha=0 digit=1 space=0 punct=0 upper=0 -&gt; '2'\n'_' alpha=0 digit=0 space=0 punct=1 upper=0 -&gt; '_'\n'x' alpha=1 digit=0 space=0 punct=0 upper=0 -&gt; 'X'\n'!' alpha=0 digit=0 space=0 punct=1 upper=0 -&gt; '!'</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>True for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>isalpha</code> / <code>isdigit</code> / <code>isalnum</code></td><td>letters / <code>0-9</code> / either</td></tr><tr><td><code>isspace</code></td><td>space, <code>\\t</code>, <code>\\n</code>, <code>\\v</code>, <code>\\f</code>, <code>\\r</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>isupper</code> / <code>islower</code></td><td>case</td></tr><tr><td><code>ispunct</code></td><td>printable, not alnum, not space</td></tr><tr><td><code>isxdigit</code></td><td><code>0-9a-fA-F</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>toupper</code> / <code>tolower</code></td><td>convert (other characters unchanged)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The <code>is…</code> functions return <em>non-zero</em> for true — not necessarily 1. That is why the sample uses <code>!!</code> to normalise.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>time.h</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;time.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    struct tm launch = {0};\n    launch.tm_year = 2026 - 1900;       /* years since 1900 */\n    launch.tm_mon = 0;                  /* 0 = January */\n    launch.tm_mday = 26;\n    launch.tm_hour = 9;\n    launch.tm_isdst = -1;               /* let mktime work out DST */\n\n    time_t t = mktime(&amp;launch);         /* normalises fields and fills tm_wday / tm_yday */\n    char buf[64];\n    strftime(buf, sizeof buf, \"%A, %d %B %Y %H:%M\", &amp;launch);\n    printf(\"%s (day %d of the year)\\n\", buf, launch.tm_yday + 1);\n\n    struct tm later = launch;\n    later.tm_mday += 40;                /* date arithmetic: mktime rolls over months */\n    time_t t2 = mktime(&amp;later);\n    strftime(buf, sizeof buf, \"%Y-%m-%d\", &amp;later);\n    printf(\"+40 days = %s, difftime = %.0f days\\n\", buf, difftime(t2, t) / 86400);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Monday, 26 January 2026 09:00 (day 26 of the year)\n+40 days = 2026-03-07, difftime = 40 days</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — current time and timing (output varies)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;time.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    time_t now = time(NULL);                       /* seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC */\n    printf(\"now: %s\", ctime(&amp;now));\n\n    clock_t start = clock();                       /* CPU time, not wall time */\n    volatile double x = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000000; i++) x += i;\n    printf(\"loop took %.3f s of CPU\\n\", (double)(clock() - start) / CLOCKS_PER_SEC);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between time() and clock()?</summary><div><p><code>time()</code> returns wall-clock calendar time (seconds since the Unix epoch). <code>clock()</code> returns the processor time used by the program, in <code>CLOCKS_PER_SEC</code> units — it does not advance while the process sleeps. For precise wall-clock intervals use POSIX <code>clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, …)</code> or C11 <code>timespec_get</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}